=== Spinor Representations

We have discussed the representation of Lorentz group on the Hilbert
space. Now we focus on the representation of Lorentz group on the
space of fields. Considering the standard model, we mainly care about
the spin 1/2 representations. The six Lorentz generators
$
    J^i := 1/2 epsilon^(i j k) J^(j k),
    quad
    K^i := J^(0 i)
$

satisfy

$
    [J^i, J^j] & = i epsilon^(i j k) J^k, \
    [J^i, K^j] & = i epsilon^(i j k) K^k, \
    [K^i, K^j] & = -i epsilon^(i j k) J^k.
$

Define the complex combinations

$
    N_i^+ := 1/2 (J^i + i K^i),
    quad
    N_i^- := 1/2 (J^i - i K^i).
$

They obey

$
    [N_i^+, N_j^+] & = i epsilon_(i j k) N_k^+, \
    [N_i^-, N_j^-] & = i epsilon_(i j k) N_k^-, \
    [N_i^+, N_j^-] & = 0.
$

Thus the complexified Lorentz algebra is two commuting copies of
$"su"(2)$, and finite-dimensional irreducible representations are
labeled by $(j_L, j_R)$.

- For the undotted $(1/2, 0)$ representation,

$
    J^i = sigma^i/2,
    quad
    K^i = -i sigma^i/2.
$

A rotation by $bold(theta)$ and boost of rapidity $bold(eta)$ are
represented by

$
    Lambda
    = exp(i (bold(theta) - i bold(eta)) dot bold(sigma) / 2)
    in "SL"(2, CC),
    quad
    det Lambda = 1.
$

An undotted spinor transforms as

$
    lambda_alpha arrow.r
    Lambda_alpha #h(0pt)^beta lambda_beta.
$
#remark([By convention the LT matrix have the first index as the row
    index as lower index and the second index as the column index as
    upper index. Always stick to this convention.])

- For the dotted $(0, 1/2)$ representation,

$
    J^i = sigma^i/2,
    quad
    K^i = +i sigma^i/2,
$

and the conjugate spinor transforms as

$
    tilde(lambda)_(dot(alpha)) arrow.r
    (Lambda^*)_(dot(alpha))#h(0pt)^(dot(beta))
    tilde(lambda)_(dot(beta)) = tilde(lambda)_dot(beta) (Lambda^dagger)^dot(beta)#h(0pt) _(dot(alpha))
$
#remark([
    One important observation is that the complex conjugate of a (1/2,
    0) representation is a (0, 1/2) representation. Thus, the dot and
    undotted indices can be viewed as related to each other with a CC.
    $
        lambda_alpha^* = tilde(lambda)_(dot(alpha)),
    $
    The lorentz transformation of the (0,1/2) representation can also
    be written as the complex conjugate of the (1/2,0) representation
    as above:
    $
        tilde(lambda)_(dot(alpha)) arrow.r
        (Lambda^*)_(dot(alpha))#h(0pt)^(dot(beta))
        tilde(lambda)_(dot(beta))
    $
    Here we also take the convention that the first index is the row
    index and the second index is the column index, we only rename
    them as dotted indices to show that they live in the (0,1/2)
    representation space.
])

Now we have the basic conventions for the spinors and spinor indices.
We can further define raising and lowering of spinor indices to define
LT invariant contractions. Dotted and undotted indices are raised and
lowered with antisymmetric tensors:
// $
//     lambda^alpha = epsilon^(alpha beta) lambda_beta,
//     quad
//     lambda_alpha = epsilon_(alpha beta) lambda^beta,
//     quad
//     epsilon^(alpha beta) epsilon_(beta gamma) = delta^alpha_gamma,
//     quad
//     epsilon^(12) = 1.
// $
$
    lambda^alpha = epsilon^(alpha beta) lambda_beta,
    quad
    lambda_alpha = epsilon_(alpha beta) lambda^beta,
    quad
    epsilon^(alpha beta) epsilon_(beta gamma)
    = delta^alpha_gamma,
    quad
    epsilon^(1 2) = 1,
    quad
    epsilon_(1 2) = -1.
$

$
    tilde(lambda)^dot(alpha)
    = epsilon^(dot(alpha) dot(beta))
    tilde(lambda)_dot(beta),
    quad
    tilde(lambda)_dot(alpha)
    = epsilon_(dot(alpha) dot(beta))
    tilde(lambda)^dot(beta),
    quad
    epsilon^(dot(alpha) dot(beta))
    epsilon_(dot(beta) dot(gamma))
    = delta^dot(alpha)_dot(gamma),
    quad
    epsilon^(dot(1) dot(2)) = 1,
    quad
    epsilon_(dot(1) dot(2)) = -1.
$
Under a Lorentz transformation, the four types of spinors transform as
$
    lambda_alpha arrow.r
    Lambda_alpha#h(0pt)^beta lambda_beta,
    quad
    lambda^alpha arrow.r
    (Lambda^(-1))_beta#h(0pt)^alpha lambda^beta,quad
    tilde(lambda)_dot(alpha) arrow.r
    (Lambda^*)_dot(alpha)#h(0pt)^dot(beta)
    tilde(lambda)_dot(beta),
    quad
    tilde(lambda)^dot(alpha) arrow.r
    ((Lambda^*)^(-1))_dot(beta)#h(0pt)^dot(alpha)
    tilde(lambda)^dot(beta).
$
#remark([
    To consist with the following conventions, it is better to use
    $Lambda^dagger$ instead of $Lambda^*$. (just take a transpose)
    Thus we can write the transformations of the dotted spinors as:
    $
        tensor(tilde(lambda), -dot(alpha)) arrow.r
        tensor(tilde(lambda), -dot(beta))
        tensor(Lambda^dagger, +dot(beta), -dot(alpha)),
        quad
        tensor(tilde(lambda), +dot(alpha)) arrow.r
        tensor((Lambda^dagger)^(-1), +dot(alpha), -dot(beta))
        tensor(tilde(lambda), +dot(beta)).
    $
    now the upper index dotted spinor behaves like a ket.
])
We can thus see that the upper and lower indices transform in the
opposite way, and the dotted and undotted indices transform in the
complex conjugate way. The inverse property may give us a chance of
defining a Lorentz invariant contraction of spinors. We first
introduce a braket notation for the spinors, such that the contraction
of vectors gives a LT invariant scalar. The spinor-helicity bra-ket
notation is

$
    chevron.l lambda| := lambda^alpha,
    quad
    |lambda chevron.r := lambda_alpha,
    quad
    [lambda| := tilde(lambda)_(dot(alpha)),
        quad
        |lambda] := tilde(lambda)^(dot(alpha)).
$

The two Lorentz-invariant contractions are

$
    chevron.l p q chevron.r
    := p^alpha q_alpha
    = epsilon^(alpha beta) p_beta q_alpha,
$
$
    [p q]
    := tilde(p)_(dot(alpha)) tilde(q)^(dot(alpha)).
$
#remark([
    One should be careful about the ordering of the contraction. In
    the $chevron.l chevron.r$ contraction, the first one have upper
    index and the second one have lower index. In the $[ ]$
    contraction, the first one have lower index and the second one
    have upper index. This is in general important due to the fact
    that lower and upper $epsilon$ are not the same and both
    antisymmetric.
])

Both are antisymmetric:

$
    chevron.l p q chevron.r = -chevron.l q p chevron.r,
    quad
    [p q] = -[q p],
    quad
    chevron.l p p chevron.r = [p p] = 0.
$

=== Momentum as a bispinor

Indeed the spin 1/2 field can be taken as a 2 component spinor field.
Moreover, we can also represent 4 vectors as composite spinors. A
four-momentum can be mapped to a $2 times 2$ matrix:

$
    p_(alpha dot(alpha))
    := p_mu sigma^mu_(alpha dot(alpha))
    = mat(
        p^0 - p^3, -p^1 + i p^2;
        -p^1 - i p^2, p^0 + p^3
    ).
$




Defining

$
    p^+ := p^0 + p^3,
    quad
    p^- := p^0 - p^3,
    quad
    p_perp := p^1 + i p^2,
$

we may also write

$
    p_(alpha dot(alpha))
    = mat(p^-, -p_perp^*; -p_perp, p^+).
$
If we do a Lorentz transformation to the 4 momentum, the bispinor
transforms as the spinors with suitable index dot and position:
(that's why this is called a bispinor not meerly a matrix)
$
    tensor(p, -alpha, -dot(alpha)) arrow.r
    tensor(Lambda, -alpha, +beta)
    tensor(p, -beta, -dot(beta))
    tensor(Lambda^dagger, +dot(beta), -dot(alpha)).
$
#remark([
    Notice that the dotted and undotted and upper and lower indices
    are defined to suite the convention of lorentz transformation. The
    index always transforms as 2 component spinors with the same
    position and doted or undotted.])

Its determinant is the Lorentz invariant

$
    det p_(alpha dot(alpha))
    = (p^0)^2 - (p^1)^2 - (p^2)^2 - (p^3)^2
    = p^2 = m^2.
$

Therefore

- for a massive momentum, $det p != 0$ and $"rank"(p) = 2$;
$
    p^2 != 0: quad
    det(p_(alpha dot(alpha))) != 0,
    quad
    rank(p_(alpha dot(alpha))) = 2,
$

- for a massless momentum, $det p = 0$ and $"rank"(p) = 1$.

$
    p^2 = 0: quad
    det(p_(alpha dot(alpha))) = 0,
    quad
    rank(p_(alpha dot(alpha))) = 1.
$
#remark([
    By convention, we write $tensor(sigma, +mu, -alpha, -dot(alpha))$
    and $tensor(overline(sigma), +mu, +dot(alpha), +alpha)$. They are
    related by
    $
        tensor(overline(sigma), +mu, +dot(alpha), +alpha)
        =
        tensor(epsilon, +alpha, +beta)
        tensor(epsilon, +dot(alpha), +dot(beta))
        tensor(sigma, +mu, -beta, -dot(beta)).
    $
    When raising or lowering both indices, their order is exchanged.
    This is to suit the convention of the matrix and the first index
    is always the row index and the second is the column index. Thus
    the momentum matrix under raising of indices is:
    $
        p^(dot(alpha) alpha )
        = p_mu tensor(overline(sigma), +mu, +dot(alpha), +alpha)
        = mat(
            p^0 + p^3, p^1 - i p^2;
            p^1 + i p^2, p^0 - p^3
        ).
    $
])



== Spinors for Massless Particles

=== Momentum and Basis Spinors

In the massless case, the rank-one matrix factorizes:
$
    p_(alpha dot(alpha))
    = lambda_alpha tilde(lambda)_(dot(alpha))
    = |p chevron.r [p|.
$
Choose the reference momentum
$
    k^mu = E (1, 0, 0, 1).
$

With the convention
$k_(alpha dot(alpha)) = k_mu sigma^mu_(alpha dot(alpha))$ and
$k^(dot(alpha) alpha) = k_mu overline(sigma)^mu^(dot(alpha) alpha)$,
we have:

$
    tensor(k, +dot(alpha), +alpha)
    = 2 E mat(1, 0; 0, 0)
    = k^dot(alpha) k^alpha.
$

Under a Lorentz transformation, the dot on the index and the position
of the index

$
    tensor(p, +dot(alpha), +alpha)
    = tensor(Lambda^dagger, +dot(alpha), -dot(beta))
    tensor(k, +dot(beta), +beta)
    tensor(Lambda, -beta, +alpha)
    = p^dot(alpha) p^alpha.
$

For a real null momentum of energy $E$ and direction $(theta, phi)$:
$
    p^mu = E (1, sin theta cos phi, sin theta sin phi, cos theta),
$
one may choose the following spinors to represent the momentum:
// $
//     |p]
//     = sqrt(2 E)
//     vec(
//         cos(theta / 2),
//         sin(theta / 2) e^(-i phi),
//     ),quad
//     ket(p)
//     = sqrt(2 E)
//     vec(
//         cos(theta / 2),
//         sin(theta / 2) e^(i phi),
//     ).
// $

$
    ket(p) = sqrt(2 E) vec(
        -e^(-i phi) sin(theta / 2),
        cos(theta / 2),
    ),
    quad
    |p] = sqrt(2 E) vec(
        cos(theta / 2),
        e^(i phi) sin(theta / 2),
    ).
$
remember the difference between bras and kets are raising and lowering
index and the difference between $ket(p)$ and $|p]$ is complex
conjugation and a raising and lowering of index. The remaining spinors
are obtained by raising and lowering indices:

$
    p_(dot(alpha))
    = epsilon_(dot(alpha) dot(beta)) p^dot(beta),
    quad
    p^alpha = (p^dot(alpha))^*,
    quad
    p_alpha = epsilon_(alpha beta) p^beta.
$
Though the momentum is invariant under little group transformations,
the spinor that constructs the momentum should be not. And it will in
fact transform covariantly under the little group by the definition of
spinor transformation. A little-group rotation around the direction of
$p$ acts as
$
    ket(p) arrow.r e^(i phi.alt / 2) ket(p),
    quad
    |p] arrow.r e^(-i phi.alt / 2)|p],
$
and leaves $p_(alpha dot(alpha)) = ket(p)[p|$ invariant. Thus,
everything is consistent. We say that the spinor $ket(p)$ has helicity
$-1/2$ and the spinor $|p]$ has helicity $+1/2$, from now on we will
use helicity weight to denote how a quantity transform under rotation
around a certain direction (most probabaly the momentum direction).

#remark([
    For a real null momentum, $ket(p)$ and $|p]$ are defined only up
    to opposite phase factors:
    $
        ket(p) arrow.r e^(i phi.alt ) ket(p),
        quad
        |p] arrow.r e^(-i phi.alt )|p].
    $
    one may see that this corresponds to the little group
    transformation of rotation around the momentum direction.
])

One now may realize that the spinors constructed here can natually be
the wave function of spin 1/2 particles (i.e. when Weyl fermion act on
vaccum gives a spinor wave function) with fixed chiralities and
helicities $u_+, u_-$.The result is that:
#remark([
    Here we use the 4 component spinors notation for convenience in
    calculation. Though it can understand as 2 Weyl spinors combined
    together.
])

$
    u_-(p) &= ket(p), quad
    u_+(p) &= |p bracket.r, quad
    v_+(p) &= ket(p), quad
    v_-(p) &= |p bracket.r, \
    overline(u)_-(p) &= bracket.l p|, quad
    overline(u)_+(p) &= bra(p), quad
    overline(v)_+(p) &= bracket.l p|, quad
    overline(v)_-(p) &= bra(p).
$
Here we abuse the notation to use 2 component spinors to represent 4
component spinors. What we really mean is that:
$
    u_-(p)
    =
    vec(
        ket(p),
        0
    ),
    quad
    u_+(p)
    =
    vec(
        0,
        |p bracket.r
    ),
$

$
    v_+(p)
    =
    vec(
        ket(p),
        0
    ),
    quad
    v_-(p)
    =
    vec(
        0,
        |p bracket.r
    ).
$
$
    overline(u)_-(p)
    =
    (
        0,
        bracket.l p|
    ),
    quad
    overline(u)_+(p)
    =
    (
        bra(p),
        0
    ),
$

$
    overline(v)_+(p)
    =
    (
        0,
        bracket.l p|
    ),
    quad
    overline(v)_-(p)
    =
    (
        bra(p),
        0
    ).
$

The $| p chevron.r$ ket always stays on the upper of left of the 4
component spinor and the $| p]$ ket always stays on the lower or right
of the 4 component spinor. Another commonly used notation is that:
$
    p_mu gamma^mu = ket(p)[p| + |p bracket.r bra(p).
$
All this series of notation makes it convenience to calculate diagrams
containing fermions.


=== Polarization Vectors

Now we turn to the polarization vectors of the massless vector fields.
We know that under Wigner's little group the polarization vectors
transform nontrivially. Thus it need more than the momentum spinors to
construct the polarization vectors. We introduce an auxiliary null
reference momentum $xi$ such that
$
    xi^2 = 0,
    quad
    ⟨p xi⟩ != 0,
    quad
    [p xi] != 0.
$


$
    epsilon^+_(alpha dot(alpha))(p, xi)
    = sqrt(2) 1 / ⟨xi p⟩ ket(xi) [p|,
$

$
    epsilon^-_(alpha dot(alpha))(p, xi)
    = sqrt(2) 1 / [p xi] (ket(p) [xi|).
$
One can see that they satisfy

$
    p dot epsilon^plus.minus = 0,
    quad
    epsilon^+ dot epsilon^- = -1.
$
Moreover, under little group rotations they transform nontrivially:
$
    epsilon^plus.minus arrow.r e^(plus.minus i phi.alt) epsilon^plus.minus.
$
This is consistent with the fact that the polarization vectors are
transverse to the momentum and have helicity $plus.minus 1$.

The reference spinor is arbitrary. Since $⟨p xi⟩ != 0$, another choice
can be written as

$
    ket(xi') = a ket(xi) + b ket(p).
$

Then

$
    epsilon^+_(alpha dot(alpha))(p, xi')
    = epsilon^+_(alpha dot(alpha))(p, xi)
    + (sqrt(2) b) / (a ⟨xi p⟩)
    p_(alpha dot(alpha)).
$

Thus changing the reference spinor shifts the polarization by a term
proportional to the momentum. The requirement that observables be
independent of $xi$ is the gauge redundancy.

For sake of calculation, we have another expression of the
polarization vectors in terms of the 4 compnent spinors and the gamma
matrices:
$
    epsilon_-^mu (p; q)
    =
    frac(
        bra(p) gamma^mu|q bracket.r,
        sqrt(2) bracket.l q p bracket.r
    ),
    quad
    epsilon_+^mu (p; q)
    =
    frac(
        bra(q) gamma^mu|p bracket.r,
        sqrt(2) chevron.l q p chevron.r
    ).
$


== Spinors for massive particles

=== Momentum and Basis Spinors

For a massive momentum,

$
    det(p_(alpha dot(alpha))) = p^2 = m^2 != 0,
$

so the rank-two bispinor can be decomposed into two rank-one matrices:

$
    p_(alpha dot(alpha))
    = l^1_(alpha dot(alpha)) + l^2_(alpha dot(alpha))
    = ket(1)[1| + ket(2)[2|,
    quad
    (l^1)^2 = (l^2)^2 = 0.
$

This decomposition is not unique. In the rest frame,

$
    k^mu = (m, 0, 0, 0),
    quad
    l_1^mu = m/2 (1, bold(n)),
    quad
    l_2^mu = m/2 (1, -bold(n)).
$

The two null rays define the spin-quantization axis (just a stupid
name for now)

$
    n^mu = 1/m (l_1^mu - l_2^mu),
    quad
    n^2 = -1,
    quad
    n dot k = 0.
$
#remark([This axis is not unique, we have a freedom of choosing an
    internal direction. One can understand as we use two massless
    particles moving in opposite directions to construct a massive
    particle momentum.
])
// Under the rotation along $bold(n)$, if we directly use the spinor
// transformation rules, we find that the spinors transform as:
// This means that they have helicity of:
//
// - $|1]$: $+1/2$
// - $ket(1)$: $-1/2$
// - $|2]$: $-1/2$
// - $ket(2)$: $+1/2$
//

Under a rotation by an angle $phi.alt$ around $bold(n)$, the two null
momenta $l_1$ and $l_2$ remain invariant. Using the spinor
transformation rules, we find
$
    |1] arrow.r e^(-i phi.alt / 2)|1],
    quad
    ket(1) arrow.r e^(i phi.alt / 2) ket(1),
    quad
    |2] arrow.r e^(i phi.alt / 2) |2],
    quad
    ket(2) arrow.r e^(-i phi.alt / 2) ket(2).
$
Thus we say that the spinors have the "helicity" of:

- $|1]$: $h = +1/2$ $ket(1)$: $h = -1/2$ $|2]$: $h = -1/2$ $ket(2)$:
    $h = +1/2$

We may notice that the helicity of 1 and 2 are opposite, this is due
to they in fact are moving in opposite directions. We take the
direction of $l_1$ as the spin-quantization axis, thus the spinors
associated with $l_2$ have opposite helicity.

#remark([
    Notice that this is not true helicity, cause it is just the "spin"
    along a chosen axis. But we split the massless momentum using this
    axis as the momentum direction, thus we call it helicity.
])

Before we notice that the massless momentum spinors naturally give us
the "wavefunction" $u_+, u_-$ (sorry for not having a better phrase)
of the Weyl fermions. Now we can also see that the massive momentum
spinors naturally give us the "wavefunction" of the Dirac fermions
with spin quantized around $n$ direction. They are:
$
    u_+ (k) = vec(-ket(2), |1]),
    quad
    u_- (k) = vec(ket(1), |2]).
$
#remark([
    Quantized around $n$ direction means that the spinor we take is
    eigenstate of the rotational generator along $n$ direction of the
    spinor representation of lorentz group.
])

Taking $n$ direction as
$bold(n) = (sin theta cos phi, sin theta sin phi, cos theta),$
we have the explicit from (up to a relative phase factor):
$
    u_+(k) = sqrt(m) vec(
        cos(theta / 2),
        e^(i phi) sin(theta / 2),
        cos(theta / 2),
        e^(i phi) sin(theta / 2),
    ),
    quad
    u_-(k) = sqrt(m) vec(
        -e^(-i phi) sin(theta / 2),
        cos(theta / 2),
        -e^(-i phi) sin(theta / 2),
        cos(theta / 2),
    ).
$

One can prove that they solve the Dirac equation in the rest frame.
Moreover, the commonly used basis for dirac spinors which is:
$
    u_+(k) = sqrt(m) vec(1, 0, 1, 0), quad u_-(k) = sqrt(m) vec(0, 1, 0, 1)
$
is just the special case of $bold(n) = (0, 0, 1)$.

// Both Weyl components describe the same "helicity" state. In fact, this
// relation can be generalized to all momentum and we will discuss about
// it below.
// #remark([
//     One should keep in mind that helicity is not well defined for
//     massive particles. Nevertheless, here we means helicity along some
//     hand chosen axis where the massive particle is combined by two
//     massless particles.
// ])

In the rest frame,

$
    k_(alpha dot(alpha))
    = m mat(1, 0; 0, 1).
$

A rotation leaves $k$ invariant, however it rotates the two null
momenta $l_1$ and $l_2$:
$
    R k R^dagger = k,
    quad
    R l^i R^dagger != l^i.
$
Thus the massive little group acts nontrivially on the two null
momenta and rotates the spin-quantization axis.

For a general momentum, we can decompose it into the following two
parts:
$
    p^mu
    = l^mu + m^2/(2 l dot q) q^mu,
    quad
    l^2 = q^2 = 0.
$
We can find that $p^2 = m^2$. A convenient spinor decomposition is
$
    ket(1) = ket(l),
    quad
    ket(2) = m/⟨q l⟩ ket(q),
    quad
    |1] = |l],
    quad
    |2] = m/[l q]|q],
$
where we use a useful identity is for any null $l$ and $q$,
$
    2 l dot q = ⟨l q⟩ [q l].
$
Moreover, we can also combine the two spinor together and amazingly
find a way to solve the dirac equation for a general momentum and get
the wavefunctions of the Dirac fermions:
$
    u_+ (p)
    = vec(
        m/⟨l q⟩ ket(q),
        |l],
    ),
    quad
    u_- (p)
    = vec(
        ket(l),
        m/[l q]|q],
    ).
$
We know that the Basis of "wavefunction" is not unique and depend on
the rest frame spinor basis choice and a boost choice. This choice of
$l,q$ decomposition implicitly chooses a basis.

// #remark([
//     This wavefunction is at decomposed in basis such that the spinor
//     are eigenvector of the spinor space rotation along $q$ direction.
//     If we choose $q$ have same spacial direction as $p$, we get the
//     helicity basis.
// ])
Now we consider the massless limit. For any fixed $q$,

$
    m^2 arrow.r 0
    quad => quad
    p^mu arrow.r l^mu.
$

The wavefunctions reduce to

$
    u_+ (p)
    = vec(
        m/⟨ l q⟩ ket(q),
        |l],
    )
    arrow.r
    vec(0, |l]),
    quad
    u_- (p)
    = vec(
        ket(l),
        m/[l q]|q],
    )
    arrow.r
    vec(ket(l), 0).
$
This is a result we are familiar with which is massless Dirac spinor
has a single nonvanishing chirality.




=== Polarization Vectors

The massive-vector polarization bispinors are

$
    epsilon^+
    = frac(|1] bra(2), sqrt(2) m),
    quad
    epsilon^-
    = frac(|2] bra(1), sqrt(2) m),
    quad
    epsilon^0
    = frac(|1] bra(1) - |2] bra(2), m).
$

They have the correct little-group transformation and obey
$
    p dot epsilon = 0.
$

We now take the massless limit. transverse polarizations remain
finite:
// $
//     epsilon^+
//     arrow.r
// frac(|l] chevron.l q|, sqrt(2) chevron.l l q chevron.r),
//     quad
//     epsilon^-
//     arrow.r
//     frac( |l chevron.r [q|, sqrt(2) [l q]).
// $
//
$
    epsilon^+
    arrow.r
    frac(
        bar.v l bracket.r chevron.l q bar.v,
        sqrt(2) chevron.l l q chevron.r
    )
    quad
    epsilon^-
    arrow.r
    frac(
        bar.v l chevron.r bracket.l q bar.v,
        sqrt(2) bracket.l l q bracket.r
    ).
$
We can see $q$ now becomes the massless reference vector. A normalized
longitudinal polarization can be written as
$
    epsilon_0^mu (p; q)
    = 1/m (
        l^mu
        - m^2/(2 l dot q) q^mu
    )
    = p^mu/m
    - m/(p dot q) q^mu.
$

Its massless limit is singular, but the divergent part is proportional
to $p^mu$ and is therefore pure gauge.

=== From polarizations to fields

In QFT, we play with fields. The vector field can be expanded in the
following terms in terms of the polarization vector.

$
    A_mu (x)
    = integral
    (dd(p, 3))/((2 pi)^3 2 E_p)
    sum_(h = plus.minus 1)
    (
        epsilon_mu^h (p)
        a_h (p)
        e^(-i p dot x)
        +
        epsilon_mu^(h *) (p)
        a_h^dagger (p)
        e^(i p dot x)
    ).
$

For

$
    ket((p, h))
    = a_h^dagger (p) ket(0),
$

the field-state overlap is

$
    bra(0) A_mu (x) ket((p, h))
    = epsilon_mu^h (p) e^(-i p dot x).
$

If the polarization is shifted by

$
    epsilon_mu^h (p)
    arrow.r
    epsilon_mu^h (p) + c(p) p_mu,
$

then, after absorbing an overall factor of $i$ into $alpha$,

$
    delta A_mu (x)
    = integral
    (dd(p, 3))/((2 pi)^3 2 E_p)
    c(p) p_mu a_h (p) e^(-i p dot x)
    = partial_mu alpha(x).
$

Hence a change of reference spinor is equivalent to the gauge
transformation

$
    A_mu arrow.r A_mu + partial_mu alpha.
$

